      trivia: "【“Clear Havana” as an invention against the tariff】The business Samuel Seidenberg began in Key West in 1867 circumvented the high tariff on finished Havana cigars and Spain’s trade restrictions by importing only the leaf and having Cuban craftsmen roll it on American soil. It is fair to say the American premium cigar industry was born of this piece of tariff arbitrage.\n【Cigar makers’ wages funded Cuban independence】The San Carlos Institute, opened in 1871 by Cuban craftsmen pooling part of their slender wages, was where José Martí rallied the exiles in 1892 and proclaimed the final phase of the independence movement. The cigar island was, at the same time, the political heart of Cuba in exile.\n【One fire gave birth to Tampa】The great fire of 1886 burnt eleven cigar factories, among them Vicente Martínez Ybor’s. Rather than rebuild, he moved to the outskirts of Tampa and opened his first factory there that April — the birth of Ybor City. Key West’s calamity begat Florida’s other cigar city."
